try. The difference is that they found—or created—environments where their communication needs were taken seriously, rather than being told they inherently couldn’t keep pace. Now, let’s bring this back to your mentee. If you believe what you just wrote—that no amount of accommodation can bridge the gap, and that quick verbal work is the default requirement for nearly all jobs—then you genuinely cannot be an effective mentor to her. Because you’ll be coaching her from the assumption that she’s innately incapable of succeeding in the vast majority of careers. That’s not a “kind reality check”; that’s a foreclosure on her future. The kindest thing you can do is wrestle with your own beliefs before passing them on to her. Ask yourself: Have I truly seen what a well-accommodated Deaf professional looks like? Or have I only seen people who were dropped into indifferent workplaces with no real support? Because those are two very different pictures, and you may be mistaking one for the other. If, after that reflection, you still feel unable to mentor her without caping her ambitions, the most decent path is to tell her honestly that you don’t feel equipped to support her goals and help her find a mentor—preferably a Deaf one—who can. That would be far kinder than dressing up low expectations as wisdom.
